+# Level 1 for threads (`--det-threads`): design note
+
+Status: prototype (track l1-threads, P-012). Builds on Level 0 (`--det`) and on the
+asyncio half of Level 1 (`docs/L1-ASYNCIO.md`, shared `sched` vocabulary, tag 9).
+
+## Problem
+
+Level 0 fixes order, seeds, clock, filesystem and network, but threads still run
+under the OS scheduler and the GIL's 5 ms switch interval. Two runs of the same test
+interleave differently, so the events of different threads reach the trace in a
+different order (Block 2: easypy's 10 thread tests fail 10/10, never with the same
+fingerprint), and races that need a rare interleaving cannot be replayed at all.
+Level 0 also has to sleep for real whenever a second thread is alive.
+
+## Mechanism: one baton, switches only at controlled points
+
+* **One managed thread runs at a time.** Every thread started with `threading.Thread`
+  (also pool workers, `threading.Timer`) while the scheduler is active is
+  *managed*: it gets a logical index (deterministic: assigned by its parent at
+  `start()`) and a private gate. It runs Python code only while it holds the baton;
+  handing the baton over is "release the next thread's gate, wait on my own".
+* **Controlled points** (the only places where the running thread can change):
+  `Lock`/`RLock` acquire and release, `Condition`/`Event`/`Semaphore`/`Barrier`
+  waits (all built on our lock, so `queue.Queue` and `concurrent.futures` follow;
+  the C `queue.SimpleQueue` is replaced by the pure-Python one), `time.sleep`,
+  `Thread.start/join/is_alive`, thread exit, and blocking I/O on sockets and
+  selectors. Optional (exploration only): a seeded **line budget** in user code
+  (`sys.settrace`, project files only) preempts at a random line, which reaches
+  races with no synchronization at all (`x += 1` lost updates).
+* **Virtual time for waits.** A sleep or a wait with a timeout becomes a timer on
+  the Level 0 virtual clock. When no thread can run, the scheduler polls I/O
+  waiters (non-blocking); if none is ready it jumps the clock to the earliest
+  timer. No real waiting unless something outside the process is involved.
+* **Deterministic I/O between threads.** A blocking `recv`/`accept`/`select` first
+  polls; if not ready the thread waits *in the scheduler* on that readiness
+  predicate. Waiters are re-polled at deterministic points (whenever the running
+  thread blocks or yields), so a loopback server thread wakes at the same point of
+  the execution in every run. Real waiting (bounded by `--det-threads-grace`) is
+  used only for genuinely external work (subprocesses, real network).
+* **Seeded choices.** Seed 0 is the *calm* profile: the running thread continues
+  until it blocks; then the longest-waiting runnable thread runs (FIFO). Any
+  other seed *explores*: at every controlled point a SplitMix64 stream decides
+  whether to switch and to whom (per-run switch probability drawn from the seed),
+  plus the optional line budget. Every choice among two or more runnable threads is
+  recorded as a `sched.thread` event `<chosen>/<n>:<stable id>` (stable id =
+  creation site of the chosen thread + per-site counter, `main` for the test
+  thread), so the verifier names the first diverging scheduling choice.
+* **Deadlock detection.** If every managed thread is blocked, no timer is pending,
+  no I/O waiter or external call is outstanding and no unmanaged thread could
+  release anything, the scheduler raises `detpy.threads.DeadlockError` in every
+  blocked thread, with each thread's index, name and the site it waits at. With
+  only I/O waiters left, it declares a deadlock after `--det-threads-deadlock`
+  real seconds (default 5) instead of hanging.
+
+## Escape hatch (C extensions and uncontrolled blocking)
+
+A managed thread that blocks inside C code (GIL released; a lock bound before
+installation, a C-extension socket, a database driver) would stall everybody. A
+detpy watchdog thread samples the baton holder: if it made no scheduling progress
+for 50 ms and the OS reports it sleeping (`/proc/self/task/<tid>/stat`), the holder
+is *escaped*: it continues outside the scheduler and the baton passes on. It rejoins
+at its next controlled point. Code can also mark a region explicitly with
+`with detpy.threads.external():`. Every escape is recorded as an `other`
+`threads.uncontrolled` event: the fingerprint then says "not deterministic here"
+instead of claiming an identical execution. Threads that existed before the
+scheduler started (or were started by C code) are unmanaged and run freely; our
+locks fall back to plain lock behaviour for them.
+
+## What it does not do (yet)
+
+* Preemption between controlled points only in exploration and only via
+  `settrace` (cost); a pure-Python busy loop with no controlled point is rescued by
+  the watchdog (escape), which is not deterministic.
+* Free-threaded CPython (3.13t) is out of scope: the baton still serialises, but
+  C-level atomics are not modelled.
+* Processes (multiprocessing, subprocess) stay external (Level 2 work).
+
+## Compared approaches
+
+| approach | determinism | cost | reach |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `sys.setswitchinterval` large | none (OS still decides who wakes) | free | — |
+| baton at controlled points (this) | full while all blocking is controlled | per lock op, plus `settrace` when preempting | races through sync objects, sleeps, I/O; data races only with the line budget |
+| record/replay of real interleaving (rr, Chess-style) | full | high, OS level | Level 2 |
